# tests/harness/giveup.rxt — [DD-14 wave A commit 3] the `gu` directive's
# own positive corpus: two cells where a typed give-up code IS reachable
# today, with no new construct, through the `engine`/`budget` directives
# this commit also adds (docs/testing.md's "The `gu` directive").
#
# Lives here rather than under a module directory because it is testing
# HARNESS VOCABULARY (does `gu` correctly score a give-up), not any
# particular regex construct's behavior — the same reason run.sh/driver.c
# themselves live in tests/harness/ rather than under tests/base/.
#
# The two patterns and budgets below drive the SAME two bounds
# tests/vm/run_vm_tests.sh's own `build()` calls do (§4/§4.5 there):
# --step-budget=50 for STEPS, --backtrack-frames=4 for FRAMES. That file
# exercises them through its own C driver (`err_steps`/`err_frames`); this
# file exercises the identical mechanism through the .rxt corpus's own
# vocabulary, which before this commit had no way to assert a give-up at
# all (§10.3).
#
# [MECH-REACH fix, lane axesfix, 2026-09-22] THE TRAILING ANCHOR IS A
# TWO-MEMBER CLASS, NOT THE BARE LITERAL 'b'/'c' run_vm_tests.sh USES,
# and that is deliberate rather than cosmetic. [OPT-ANCHOR-VM]/[OPT-ENDWIN]/
# [OPT-REQBYTE] (D119 batch 1) landed a whole-window `memchr` pre-check
# for a pattern's one NECESSARY byte (src/opt/reqbyte.c); for the bare
# '(a*)*b'/'((a)|b)*c' shapes that byte is 'b'/'c', and BOTH witness
# subjects below are deliberately 'b'/'c'-free (that is what forces the
# catastrophic backtracking these cells exist to exhaust a budget on). A
# `.rxt` `pattern` block has NO directive that can deny a compiler axis
# (`pcrec --list-schema`: the `pcrec` raw-flags line is `config`-scope
# only, never `block`-scope; `RXTFLAGS` is the format's own escape hatch
# for exactly this, but it is a RUN-WIDE env var with no per-file/per-block
# form) — so unlike run_vm_tests.sh's own copies of these two witnesses,
# which build with an explicit `-fno-req-byte`, this file cannot deny the
# axis and reach its VM under bare default flags.
#
# The fix is at the PATTERN, not the flag: [OPT-REQBYTE] itself declines
# on a class with more than one member (src/opt/reqbyte.c's own "safe
# direction" arm — a class it cannot reduce to a single byte earns the
# empty set, which disables the memchr entirely). Swapping the trailing
# literal for a two-member class the subject still never satisfies
# ('[bc]', '[cd]') keeps the identical catastrophic-backtracking shape
# and the identical subjects/budgets, while making [OPT-REQBYTE] decline
# for the WHOLE pattern regardless of axis flags — VERIFIED via
# `--pattern '(a*)*[bc]'`/`--pattern '((a)|b)*[cd]' -fcomments`: both stamp
# `RX_REQ_BYTE "none"`. This changes no directive, no line count and no
# block count, so it moves none of tests/rxtsource/run_rxtsource_tests.sh's
# RUNSH_*/CENSUS_* pins or verify_rxt.py's `gu`-line census.

pattern (a*)*[bc]
engine vm
budget steps=50
budget frames=4096
gu steps "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"

pattern ((a)|b)*[cd]
engine vm
budget frames=4
gu frames "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
